---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro';
/* Showcase (Nerd Apply) is out of the scene for now. The component is
   untouched in src/components/sections/. Putting it back is this import, the
   pixel field and `data-scene-next` layer below, and hero.ts already resumes
   the hero → Showcase phase when `[data-scene-next]` is in the page. */
/* Overclock is out of the page as a chapter of its own: it is the first item
   of Our work now, opening and all — see WorkCategories.astro. The component
   is untouched in src/components/sections/, and src/scripts/overclock.ts
   returns a no-op when it cannot find `[data-project="overclock"]`. */
/* Bedford and CELPIP are out of the scene until their case studies exist.
   A chapter that hands over to a page nobody can read yet is a promise the
   site cannot keep. The components are untouched in
   src/components/sections/; putting them back is this import, their two
   blocks below, and their phases in hero.ts. */
/* Credibility is out of the page for now. The component is untouched in
   src/components/sections/, and src/scripts/credibility.ts already returns a
   no-op when it cannot find `[data-cred]`, so nothing else had to change to
   take it out — except the exit field's colour and the trigger note below,
   both of which named it explicitly. */
import WorkCategories from '../components/sections/WorkCategories.astro';
import TwoWays from '../components/sections/TwoWays.astro';
import Included from '../components/sections/Included.astro';
import Clients from '../components/sections/Clients.astro';
import Testimonials from '../components/sections/Testimonials.astro';
import TestimonialsFloat from '../components/sections/TestimonialsFloat.astro';
import TestimonialsSphere from '../components/sections/TestimonialsSphere.astro';
/* Journal is out of the page for now. The component is untouched in
   src/components/sections/, and src/scripts/journal.ts already returns a
   no-op when it cannot find `[data-jr]`. Putting it back is this import and
   the block below. The nav "Blog" link comes back with it. */
import Footer from '../components/layout/Footer.astro';
---

<Base>
  {/*
    Two chapters, one scroll scene — hero and Overclock. Showcase (Nerd Apply)
    is out for now; putting it back restores the middle beat. src/scripts/hero.ts
    switches this into pinned mode (layers stacked absolutely on top of each
    other) and drives every handover as phases of the same extended pin:
      hero → Overclock       pixel reveal, scattered, in Overclock's ground
                              colour — then the layer swaps in behind it
      Overclock → Our work   the same arrival: Overclock recedes, a cream
                              field scatters, then Our work fades in on it.
                              The pin ends as that finishes.
    Without JS — or with reduced motion — the chapters stay in normal flow and
    read top to bottom, no pin, no crossfades.
  */}
  {/* Out of the scene, and that is the point of it.

      The hero used to be the scene's first layer, which made its opening one
      more step in a stepped sequence: a scroll, and the chapter changed. The
      hero's own reveal is not a chapter change — it is one continuous thing
      the reader scrubs, so it has a pin and a scrubbed trigger of its own (see
      initHeroReveal in src/scripts/hero.ts) and the stepped scene begins after
      it, at Overclock. */}
  <Hero />

  {/* Overclock used to stand here, pinned, and dissolve into Our work. It is
      Our work's first item now: the film still arrives small on black and
      grows to the screen, and from there it runs as the list does. */}

  {/*
    After Overclock. It runs to about eight screens of its own.

    The handover into it is a crossfade now — Overclock fades out as this fades
    in — rather than the cream tile field the stepped scene used to scatter
    over it. See src/scripts/overclock.ts. What follows was written for that
    parks it over the viewport for that step. After the scene's pin it is
    ordinary page, scrolled at whatever rate the reader scrolls.

    One category at a time, full screen, from the Figma "Animation" frame: a
    sticky photograph per category with the copy scrolling in front of it. It
    holds nothing — the scene's pin is the last thing on this page that takes
    the scrollbar. The ground crossfade lives in
    src/scripts/workCategories.ts; the sticking is the stylesheet's.
  */}
  {/* The join between Overclock and Our work is not an element any more.

      It was: a black panel, fixed over the window, that rose from the bottom
      and then faded. Two things were wrong with it. It had a hard top edge, so
      what the reader saw was a black rectangle sliding up the screen and
      cutting Overclock in half along a straight line. And it did not stop
      Overclock moving — it only covered it, so the chapter was still being
      scrolled away underneath.

      What replaced it has no element and nothing that travels. Overclock stays
      exactly where it is, pinned, and dissolves from its bottom edge upwards
      behind a very soft gradient, while Our work — already parked underneath
      it — fades up. See the rules below and initOverclockHandover in
      src/scripts/overclock.ts. */}
  <WorkCategories />

  {/*
    Same plain-scroll treatment as the section above it, which it used to be.
    Its one-shot entrance lives in src/scripts/twoWays.ts.
  */}
  {/* The pixel field that hands Our work over to Two ways in.

      Declared here rather than made in script, for the same reason the paint
      order below is: it has to sit above Our work (z-index 1) and above
      Overclock (2) and below the header, and that is a fact about the page
      rather than about the animation. The tiles inside it are the script's —
      how many there are depends on the window. See initWorkPixels in
      src/scripts/workCategories.ts. */}
  <div class="pixels" data-work-pixels aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <TwoWays />

  {/* What both models include, and the ask. It is pulled up one window under
      Two ways in's last screen so the offer panel can dissolve while this is
      already rising into view behind it — see initTwoWays. The client band
      below it shares this ground, so the two read as one run. */}
  <Included />

  {/*
    A band rather than a chapter — ruled top and bottom, one row tall, and the
    only thing on the page that moves without being scrolled. It introduces the
    section under it, so the two are adjacent on purpose. No script: the loop is
    a CSS animation over a list rendered twice.
  */}
  <Clients />

  {/*
    Chapter 8, three times. The "Testimonial section" axis in
    src/config/variations.ts picks between them and each stylesheet
    display:none's itself when it is not the pick, so only one is ever on the
    page — all three are rendered because the choice is the reader's, made in
    the browser, and this site is built once.

    Film wall (the default): pins itself for a few screens — one film alone,
    then the title over it, then a wall of testimonials scrolling up under the
    title. src/scripts/testimonials.ts.

    Scrolled scatter: nothing is pinned. A tall section with the title stuck
    in the middle of it and six tiles at the frame's own positions down it,
    all of it moved by the scroll itself. It ends on the footer's photograph,
    which opens out from a card into the footer's own band —
    src/scripts/testimonialsFloat.ts for the entrances, and
    src/scripts/founderQuote.ts for the opening, because that picture belongs
    to the footer.

    Orbit grid: the founders as tiles on a sphere that turns by itself and
    under a drag. Pressing one does not open a dialog over it — that tile
    flies out of the rotation into the read and the quote arrives beside it,
    with the arrows and the keyboard walking between founders from there.
    src/scripts/testimonialsSphere.ts.

    Without JS, or under reduced motion, any of them is a title over a grid of
    films in ordinary flow.
  */}
  <Testimonials />
  <TestimonialsFloat />
  <TestimonialsSphere />

  {/* Closing block, in two sections: the founder quote on its photograph,
      then the ink band. See Footer.astro. */}
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  /* The pixel field for the Our work / Two ways in join. Painted in Two ways
     in's own ground, so the moment the field is complete the screen is already
     the colour of the section arriving — what the second half of the reveal
     uncovers is that section's content, not its background.

     Hidden until the script picks it up, so a page without JS never has a
     sheet of tiles in it. The grid's tracks and its children are the script's
     to write; everything about where it sits in the page is here. */
  .pixels {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 3;
    display: grid;
    pointer-events: none;
    visibility: hidden;
  }

  .pixels :global(span) {
    /* A hair over a whole tile, to close the sub-pixel seams a grid of
       hundreds of boxes otherwise shows as a faint lattice. */
    width: calc(100% + 1px);
    height: calc(100% + 1px);
    background: var(--color-bg-twoways);
    opacity: 0;
  }

  /* Two cards runs Two ways in on black, so the field is black too — the
     same rule as above: the tiles are the ground of the section arriving. */
  :global(html[data-var-offer='cards']) .pixels :global(span) {
    background: var(--color-bg-included);
  }

  /* --- The Overclock / Our work join ---------------------------------------

     Both halves of it are here because it is one arrangement between two
     sections, and neither section can describe it alone.

     PAINT ORDER. Our work is pulled up underneath Overclock's last screen (see
     `--cat-join` in WorkCategories.astro), so for one window of scroll the two
     sections occupy the same place. Document order would put the later one on
     top; these two numbers say otherwise. The hero stays at 3 above both — it
     overhangs Overclock by the tail of its own gradient.

     THE WIPE. Overclock is masked away from its bottom edge upwards. A very
     wide feather, because the whole point is that there is no edge to see: at
     55% of the section's height the ramp is longer than half the screen, so
     what the reader gets is a dissolve that happens to start low rather than a
     line that travels.

     Only while the join is running — `data-oc-join` is put on by the script
     when the reader reaches it. A mask is a compositing layer, and a
     full-screen film does not need one for the several screens before this. */
  /* A FLOOR under the whole ladder, and the fixed footer is why it exists.

     That footer is `position: fixed` at z-index 0, behind the page. A section
     that never states a rank of its own paints at `auto` — which is the same
     level as 0, decided by tree order, and the footer is the last element on
     the page. So every section that had nothing to say about z-index was
     losing to it: the client band, 169px of it, went transparent and the
     founder's photograph showed through the middle of the page.

     Stated on the parent rather than section by section, and deliberately at
     low specificity: a component's own rule carries its cid attribute and beats
     this, so the ladder below is untouched and this only catches whatever has
     not spoken for itself. `main > section` and not `main > *`, because the
     footer block and its spacer are divs and must stay where they are. */
  :global(main > section) {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }


  /* THE LADDER, in one place, because it is one arrangement.

     Every join on this page works by one section painting over the next until
     something takes it away — a mask, a field of tiles, a fade — so each
     chapter has to out-rank the one after it for as long as that lasts.
     Reading down the page: the hero overhangs Overclock by the tail of its own
     gradient, Overclock is held over Our work through its dissolve, Our work
     over Two ways in through the pixel reveal, and Two ways in over What both
     models include through the fade. Document order gives the opposite of
     that, which is why every one of these is stated.

     One exception, at the foot: What both models include out-ranks Two ways in
     rather than the other way round. Every join above it hands over from a
     screen that is parked and waiting; that one arrives under its own steam,
     over a screen of the same black it is itself, so it covers rather than
     being uncovered. The reason is in Included.astro, next to the number.

     Overclock is the only one set from here, because it has no stylesheet of
     its own — its layout is the shared ProjectSection. The rest each carry
     their own number, in their own file, next to the join it is there for:
     hero 4 (Hero.astro), Overclock 3, Our work 2 (WorkCategories.astro), Two
     ways in 1 (TwoWays.astro), and What both models include takes the ground
     floor. Setting them from here instead would have lost to those files'
     scoped selectors anyway — an Astro component's own rules carry its cid
     attribute, and a bare `:global(.hero)` does not.

     The numbers themselves carry no meaning beyond their order. */


  /* The scene's stylesheet is gone with the scene.

     What stood here dressed a pinned, gesture-stepped sequence: stacked
     full-viewport chapter layers, two grids of tiles for the handovers between
     them, a zero-height marker for the pin's release, and the rules that hid
     all of it once the sequence was spent. None of those elements are in the
     page any more — the hero and Overclock each pin and scrub on their own,
     and everything between them is ordinary scrolling page. */
</style>
